Revision history for Perl extension NetAddr::IP

{{$NEXT}}

    - Fix GH#138: $NetAddr::IP::netlimit holds the netlimit() exponent,
      so local scopes it. The function still returns 2**n.

    - Fix GH#138: Name the package variables local can scope: the IPv6
      case, name lookup, and the old nth() behaviour. :old_storable has
      no variable and stays process-wide.

    - Document new_no() as the constructor for IPv4 text with a leading
      zero, read as decimal.

    - Fix GH#162: Without Socket6, inet_pton(AF_INET) now accepts only four
      decimal parts 0-255 with dots. Before, it used inet_aton and also
      took short forms like "10.1", single numbers like "0", and host names.
      Now it matches Socket6 and core Socket on all hosts. Tests in t/031
      were added.

    - Fix GH#163: Pure Perl now measures packed arguments in bytes, not
      characters. Before, a string of wide characters (above 0xFF) that had
      the "right" count would pass the length checks. Those calls then gave
      wrong answers or died inside UtilPP. Now they croak "Wide character in
      NetAddr::IP::Util::func" at the caller's line. A UTF-8 string that
      holds only bytes still works. Tests in t/076 were updated.

4.080_06  2026-10-22 23:19:25+11:00 Australia/Sydney (TRIAL RELEASE)

    - Fix GH#160: Refuse a packed argument holding a character above 0xFF in
      the NetAddr::IP::InetBase functions that measure one, as the XS
      functions of NetAddr::IP::Util already do. The length checks counted
      characters, so 16 such characters passed the 16 byte check. Then
      ipv6_n2x, ipv6_n2d and inet_ntoa returned a wrong answer, as did
      ipv6_ntoa without Socket6, and from perl 5.28 isIPv4, isAnyIPv4 and
      isNewIPv4 died inside InetBase.pm. Each now croaks with
      "Wide character in" and the full name of the function that measures
      the argument, at the caller's line. A string upgraded to UTF-8 is read
      as its bytes, so ipv6_ntoa with Socket6 converts one rather than dying
      in Socket6::inet_ntop.

++ b/lib/NetAddr/IP/InetBase.pm

    - Take no leading zero in any part of an IPv4 address, and read the
      whole string as the address in the InetBase and Util text functions.
      A part such as the 010 in 192.0.2.010 is octal 8 to the C library's
      inet_aton and decimal 10 to a person. Through gethostbyname, inet_aton
      and new() read it as octal, while ipv6_aton and inet_any2n read it as
      decimal, so the same text named two addresses. Such text is now not an
      address anywhere except new_no(), which reads it as decimal as before.
      The patterns of fillIPv4 and of the dotted quad tail in ipv6_aton
      ended in $, which matches before a final newline, so one trailing
      newline was ignored on some forms and not others. They now end in \z,
      and inet_aton and naip_gethostbyname return undef for text with
      whitespace without asking the resolver. The constructors keep
      stripping surrounding whitespace from the address, as they have since
      4.080_01 (GH#5), now strip it from a separate mask as well, and under
      :aton keep the ends only of a string of four or sixteen bytes. A
      prefix length keeps its leading zeros, so 192.0.2.0/024 is still
      192.0.2.0/24. Against 4.079 these change:
      call                              4.079              now
      inet_aton("192.0.2.1\n")          192.0.2.1          undef
      inet_aton("192.0.2.010")          192.0.2.8          undef
      ipv6_aton("::ffff:192.0.2.1\n")   ::ffff:192.0.2.1   undef
      inet_any2n("192.0.2.010")         ::192.0.2.10       undef
      new("010")                        0.0.0.10/32        undef
      new("192.0.2.010")                192.0.2.8/32       undef
      new("::ffff:192.0.2.010")         ::ffff:192.0.2.10  undef
      new("192.0.2.0", "24\n")          undef              192.0.2.0/24

    - Fix GH#39: Coalesce no longer returns an empty list for a list holding
      both IPv4 and IPv6 subnets. The families are now coalesced separately,
      with the same mask length applied to each, and the IPv4 results come
      before the IPv6 results. A list of one family is unchanged.

    - Measure the argument of ipv6_ntoa in ipv6_ntoa itself, so a length
      other than 16 bytes is reported by ipv6_ntoa, at the caller's line, on
      every host. The argument went straight to inet_ntop, whose error named
      Socket6::inet_ntop and a line inside InetBase.pm where Socket6 is
